-------
ASSISTANT I can see `llm-agent/config.nix` and `llm-agent/setup.nix` from earlier in the chat. To wire up the QEMU sandbox properly, it would help to also add:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT 1. **`flake.nix` / `flakeModule.nix`** — to see how modules are composed and where `perSystem` outputs land (e.g. `packages` or `apps`), so the VM launcher can be exposed as a dev tool.
ASSISTANT 2. **`llm-providers/config.nix` and `llm-providers/prelude.nix`** — since `provider-type` comes from there, I need to know its shape (API key, endpoint, model name) to pass provider config into the VM.
ASSISTANT 3. **`lite-llm/config.nix` / `lite-llm/setup.nix`** — if the agent connects through LiteLLM, this shows the existing pattern for a similar tool I can mirror.
ASSISTANT 4. **`presets/flakeModule.nix`** and **`prelude/config.nix`** — to understand your module conventions (e.g. how `development-tools` options are aggregated).
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT A quick note on what I see so far:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - `config.nix` maps each `llm-agent` attrset entry into a `perSystem` import of `setup.nix`, passing `llm-agent-name` and `llm-agent` (the option subtree) as module args.
ASSISTANT - `setup.nix` is currently a stub (`x = 42;`, empty config) — so the VM/agent logic is entirely to be built there.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Once you add the files above, I can sketch the `setup.nix` that:
ASSISTANT - builds a NixOS VM (via `qemu-vm.nix` or a custom QEMU wrapper derivation),
ASSISTANT - injects the agent as a systemd service inside the guest,
ASSISTANT - forwards the provider config (from `llm-agent.provider`) into the guest environment.
